+**EMPIRICAL RESULT (probe BUILT + RUN 2026-06-28, `scripts/experiments/arc_frontier_tooluse_probe.py`):** the cheapest-decisive probe was built and run. A FRONTIER driver (codex/gpt-5.5) drives a SANDBOXED tool loop over the EXISTING subsystems — `explore` (random salient steps), `try_actions` (DIRECTED: execute an exact action sequence from reset, records transitions, reports level-up), `induce` (LOCAL Qwen3.5-9B-MTP, held CONSTANT vs baseline so the ONLY treatment variable is codex-DRIVING), `plan` (BFS in induced model), `execute`. Baseline = one-shot induce+BFS. Anti-cheat: driver runs `codex exec -s read-only` in an empty `/tmp` CWD, JSON-only, tool shim never returns source, post-hoc source-read scan (`cheated=False`). **RESULT on re86 + ft09 (both `fair_shot=True`):** the frontier driver with correctly-aimed directed interaction (re86: 104 coord-clicks + 26 try_actions / 471 env steps; ft09: 117 coord-clicks + 25 try_actions / 341 env steps) surfaced **ZERO first-level-ups** and **never beat the one-shot baseline** (both arms fail; baseline induce acc ≈ 0 on both → the plan/execute arm was non-viable, the driver correctly routed to directed `try_actions`). The first-contact win is **not discoverable by the agent's own attempts even when a frontier model drives directed clicks where it aims** — consistent with `WALL_IS_HIDDEN_STATE` (interaction-dependent hidden win-state, upstream of LLM orchestration). The deployable local 9B certainly cannot if the frontier driver cannot. The ~12% prior is thus REALIZED as a negative on the tested games. **HONEST LIMITATIONS:** n=2 games (suggestive, not a universal claim); the local-Qwen induce backend is weak (acc≈0 on both grids), so this tests the FRAMING (does tool-DRIVING help) more than the induce arm; verifier `accuracy` is full-grid-match (read `cell_recall`+`new_changed` alongside — an early ft09 run showed a vacuous identity-fit acc=1.0 on a never-changing grid, corrected on re-run). Two adversarial sub-agent reviews drove harness fixes before this result was trusted: a nested-args JSON-parser bug (false-null #1: 24 no-op turns) and a flat-schema CLICK-coordinate stripping bug (>50% of clicks misdirected to (0,0) on the first ft09/re86 runs) — both fixed and re-run. Artifacts `results/arc_frontier_tooluse_probe_{re86,ft09}.json` (adversarial_verify clean; `solve_provenance=development_proxy`; `off_live_path=True`; `verifier_is_oracle=False`). **NET: the operator's tool-use/MCP lever is now BUILT, RUN, and ceiling-negative on 2 games — do NOT re-build it as a scored-agent path without a genuinely new way to surface the hidden win-state. The dev-convenience use (frontier tool-loop to author GameAdapter deltas) remains positive-EV and untested.**
 
